<!--
  /styling — one way to style, and no way out.

  This page used to end with a section called "The escape hatch" that taught a
  raw <style> block "scoped to the shadow root". All three of those are gone:
  <style> is a compile error everywhere outside the compiler's own L1 elements,
  and there is no shadow root. The page taught the reader to defeat the language.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">Components</p>
  <h1>Styling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    <code>sx</code> is a closed vocabulary of design-system atoms. Not a
    shorthand for CSS — a <em>subset</em>, on purpose. A value that is not on the
    system does not compile.
  </p>

  <code-block code={code.sx}></code-block>

  <h2>States are a prefix</h2>
  <p>
    <code>hover:</code>, <code>focus:</code>, <code>focus-within:</code>,
    <code>active:</code>, <code>disabled:</code>, <code>current:</code>,
    <code>forced-colors:</code>. A state is a prefix on an atom, not a rule in a
    stylesheet somewhere else.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.states}></code-block>
  <p>
    <code>current:</code> keys off <code>aria-current</code> — the attribute you
    set for assistive tech anyway, doing double duty as the style hook. The
    product switcher at the top of this page is four links in a
    <code>row</code>; the filled pill is
    <code>current:bg:primary current:fg:primary-foreground</code>.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.current}></code-block>

  <h2>Tones, not colors</h2>
  <p>
    You do not pick a red. You declare a <strong>tone</strong>, and the framework
    resolves <code>--tone-fg</code>, <code>--tone-bg</code>,
    <code>--tone-border</code> and <code>--tone-ring</code> for the subtree. That
    is why a destructive button in one app matches a destructive alert in
    another — and why swapping the theme repaints both.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.tones}></code-block>

  <h2>Structure is not styling</h2>
  <p>
    <code>sx</code> has no <code>flex-direction</code>, no
    <code>align-items</code>, no <code>grid-template-columns</code> — and that is
    not a gap. Structure belongs to the layout keywords, where the axis is the
    name and there is nothing to get backwards. Self-sizing is different:
    <code>grow</code> says how much of the parent's spare space an element
    claims — the pane atom behind every sidebar-plus-content layout — and it
    is sx because it describes the element, not its children.
  </p>
  <p>
    Raw <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> are permitted
    for the same reason: with classes and CSS closed, a box can only say what
    <code>sx</code> can say — which makes it a shape on the system, not a hole
    in it.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.layout}></code-block>
  <p>
    Borders can name a side, because a topbar's rule and a rail's edge are the
    two commonest pieces of chrome there are:
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.sides}></code-block>

  <h2>There is no escape hatch</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part that matters, and it is the part every other system gets
    wrong. <strong>Authored CSS is a compile error.</strong> Not discouraged, not
    linted with a warning — an error, in every SUI file, with or without a config.
    The only exception is the ~40 L1 elements inside the compiler, because they
    <em>are</em> the styling layer.
  </p>
  <p>
    An escape hatch is not a safety valve; it is where the design system goes to
    die. One component reaches for a raw rule, then ten do, and now the
    "system" is a suggestion. Every project that has ever shipped a
    <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> escape has this story.
  </p>
  <alert tone="warning">
    <strong>If you cannot express something, that is a conversation — not a
    hatch.</strong> Three times while writing these docs we hit something
    <code>sx</code> "could not do", and every time the answer was that the
    element already existed (<code>&lt;navbar&gt;</code>), or the contract
    already existed (<code>tone</code>), or the rule already existed
    (<code>code</code>). The reflex to reach for CSS is almost always a failure
    to read the standard library. When it genuinely is a gap — per-side borders
    were one — the language grows a token, not a hole.
  </alert>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/elements">← Elements</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/theming">Theming →</a>
  </row>
</column>
